iminalization  stopping and holding vessels out at  Steve denying access to ports this  obviously has a hugely negative effect  on the asylum seekers that are on the  vessels Malta and Italy are on the front  line of Europe's migration crisis and  they share the same complaint they say  they have not received adequate support  from the European Union to deal with a  number of people who have for years been  arriving on their shores the conditions  that caused them to flee war poverty and  hunger remained largely unaddressed and  there is little sign that conditions  will improve and in a seemingly  never-ending saga of human misery it is  the people smuggling networks that have  profited charging vulnerable people  thousands of dollars for a dangerous  passage to Europe meanwhile European  governments wary of their own  anti-immigration movements are reluctant  to be seen be encouraging asylum seekers  and so the dilemma continues the EU has  given millions of dollars to Libya's  Tripoli based government to intercept  vessels leaving the coast but with  little effect with more than 54,000  people arriving in Europe by boat so far  this year  the Maltese authorities say that there  needs to be a different approach to this  humanitarian emergency  we welcome the authorities shown by some  Member States with regards to the  relocation of asylum seekers but of  course being these dock agreements it  means that we are sort of atamira see of  the will of the Member States the dream  of a better life we almost certainly  encourage many more to make the perilous  journey and unless the people smuggling  networks who prey in them are seriously  tackled they will continue to be left  vulnerable of course the challenge in  this is that whatever their agreements  they come up with they're going to have  to try to convince other EU Member  States to be able to take on the extra  load that has been an incredibly  contentious issue as speck  for member states such as Hungary and  